        <section id="what-is-a-firewall?"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          What is a Firewall?
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Firewalls provide protection from cyber security attackers by shielding a computer system or network from malicious or unnecessary attention. They are the first line of defence against attack and have been so for over 25 years. A firewall can be made up of hardware software or both. A firewall acts as a barrier between a trusted system or network and outside connections such as the internet. It does that by monitoring the incoming and outgoing network traffic, deciding when it needs to be allowed through or blocked, according to a defined set of security rules.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u5_s3_p1_image1.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
        <section id="key-functions-of-a-firewall"  class="t-bg-supporting3">
          
            <h2>Key functions of a firewall</h2>
              <p>The main security functions of a firewall are packet filtering, which inspects traffic at the packet level, and acting as a proxy, providing security measures at application level. However, the term 'firewall' is now applied to a wide range of technologies with different methods of operation and objectives.</p>
<p>Let’s look at the different types of firewall in more detail and the advantages and disadvantages of each.</p>

              
              
<div class="gallery-container">
  <div role="group" class="gallery no-js">
    <ul>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Packet Filtering Firewalls
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>These are the simplest type of firewall and just look at the source and destination IP and ports. This allows admins to create access control lists that state which devices are allowed to communicate and on which ports. They are simple and cost efficient to implement but because they don’t look at the content of the packets being sent, they should only be used on semi-trusted networks such as internal networks.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u5_s3_p1_slide1.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Stateful Inspection Firewalls
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Stateful inspection firewalls go further than Packet Filtering Firewalls by monitoring the entire session of a connection which gives greater context around what is connecting to what. This however, is heavier on resources and can have a negative impact on networking times.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u5_s3_p1_slide2.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Next-Generation Firewall (NGFW)
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Next-generation firewalls combine stateful inspection and Packet Filtering. This gives greater insights and awareness of what’s going on between networks. However, they are much costlier and have a bigger impact on the network speed due to the time and resources needed to inspect each message before it’s allowed through.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u5_s3_p1_slide3.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Web Application Firewall (WAF)
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Web application firewalls are used to filter traffic to a web application such as an eCommerce website. These firewalls are used specifically to address the threats that are common to websites. Websites are commonly exploited by application layer attacks such as SQL injections which look for poorly written code. They look for certain behaviours and terminate that traffic before it reaches the website. They can also be used to prevent distributed denials of service (DDoS) which work by sending a huge amount of fake traffic at a website to overload it and make it unavailable for legitimate user traffic.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u5_s3_p1_slide4.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
